    "https://www.fairmontstatejobs.com/postings/12161": {
      "title": "Director of Enterprise Solutions",
      "description": "Fairmont State University Portal | Director of Enterprise Solutions Skip to Main Content Office of Human Resources 1201 Locust Avenue, Fairmont, West Virginia 26554 Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Director of Enterprise Solutions Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Summary Working Title Director of Enterprise Solutions Type of Position Staff - Full Time Department Enterprise Application Services Appointment Length July - June (12 mo) Benefits Eligible? Yes Published Salary Requested Part-Time Staff Hours NA F.T.E. 1.00 Grant Funded Position? No If temporary (or grant funded) position, indicate ending date Description of Job Duties and Responsibilities Description of Job Duties and Responsibilities The Director of Enterprise Solutions reports directly to the CIO and provides institution-wide leadership for strategy, governance, lifecycle management, and integration of enterprise applications that support the University’s academic and administrative operations. The position develops and maintains a comprehensive enterprise application portfolio, coordinates application contracts, renewals, licensing, and vendor relationships, and identifies opportunities to reduce application redundancy, improve utilization, control costs, and lower institutional risk. With support from the Assistant Director of Enterprise Solutions, the Director leads the mapping, documentation, and continuous improvement of business processes across campus units to ensure enterprise technology investments align with institutional needs, data standards, operational requirements, and strategic priorities. The position works closely with the CIO , Data Governance Committee, IT staff, and functional offices to establish clear application ownership, governance, and lifecycle plans. The Director serves as a key leader for major enterprise system modernization initiatives, including replacement of the University’s Enterprise Resource Planning ( ERP ) and Human Capital Management ( HCM ) environments, as well as project management of application replacements/upgrades from aged or no longer supported systems, and application integrations and workflows. The position coordinates requirements, business process design, solution evaluation, implementation planning, integration strategy, testing, data conversion readiness, risk management, vendor performance, and transition to sustainable operations. The Director also oversees the architecture, documentation, reliability, security coordination, and lifecycle management of application integrations and data exchanges. Enterprise Application Portfolio Management Create and maintain the authoritative inventory of enterprise applications, including each system’s purpose, business owner, technical owner, users, vendor, contract, licensing model, cost, renewal date, data classification, integrations, dependencies, lifecycle status, and institutional risk. Establish and administer an application lifecycle framework covering request, evaluation, acquisition, implementation, operation, enhancement, renewal, replacement, retirement, and records disposition. Lead periodic application portfolio reviews to assess business value, utilization, service quality, technical health, security considerations, accessibility, supportability, total cost, and alignment with institutional priorities. Identify redundant, duplicative, overlapping, underutilized, unsupported, or high-risk applications and develop recommendations for consolidation, standardization, replacement, or retirement. Develop application roadmaps and standards that support transparent prioritization and investment decisions. Ensure that new application requests are evaluated against existing institutional capabilities before additional products or services are acquired. Application Research, Evaluation, and User Needs Analysis Coordinate intake and discovery activities for user and departmental application requests, documenting the business problem, current process, desired outcomes, affected users, functional requirements, data needs, reporting needs, integrations, constraints, priority, and measures of success. Work with requesting units to distinguish required capabilities from preferred features and to validate requirements with process owners, subject-matter experts, data stewards, accessibility reviewers, and responsible technical teams. Research existing institutional applications to determine whether a need can be met through configuration, expanded use, process improvement, training, or integration before evaluating an additional product. Conduct structured market research to identify viable commercial, Software as a Service, shared-service, and institutionally supported application options that could meet documented user needs. Validate vendor responses and demonstration results, document gaps and assumptions, and coordinate follow-up regarding configuration, integrations, data conversion, security, accessibility, support, implementation, and contractual requirements. Lead the research and evaluation of new application solutions in response to documented user, departmental, and institutional business needs. Work with requesting units to clarify the business problem, functional requirements, desired outcomes, user population, data needs, integration requirements, constraints, and measures of success before evaluating products. Develop comparative analyses of application alternatives addressing functional fit, usability, accessibility, security, privacy, data ownership, integration capability, technical architecture, implementation effort, vendor support, service levels, scalability, lifecycle risk, and alignment with institutional standards. Compare licensing models, implementation costs, recurring costs, internal resource requirements, integration costs, training needs, transition costs, and total cost of ownership for shortlisted solutions. Coordinate product demonstrations, vendor responses, proof-of-concept activities, reference checks, evaluation scoring, and stakeholder feedback using consistent criteria and documented requirements. Prepare decision briefs, comparison matrices, risk summaries, and recommendations for the CIO . Maintain documentation of evaluated solutions, selection criteria, findings, decisions, and reasons for approval, deferral, consolidation, or rejection to support transparency and future portfolio decisions. Contracts, Renewals, Licensing, and Vendor Management Coordinate the review and administration of enterprise application contracts, renewals, subscriptions, maintenance agreements, licensing, and vendor commitments with the CIO . Maintain a centralized renewal calendar and planning process that provides sufficient time to evaluate utilization, performance, pricing, risk, alternatives, and institutional requirements before renewal decisions are due. Monitor software licensing and subscription utilization; identify unused, excess, misaligned, or noncompliant licensing; and recommend right-sizing or reallocation opportunities. Support contract negotiations by documenting technical, integration, data, security, accessibility, implementation, support, service-level, transition, and exit requirements. Establish vendor performance measures and coordinate service reviews, escalation, corrective action, and renewal recommendations for strategic application providers. Document total cost of ownership, multi-year financial commitments, implementation costs, integration costs, and resource requirements associated with enterprise application decisions. Business Process Management and Institutional Process Mapping Lead the development and maintenance of an institution-wide business process inventory covering major academic, student, financial, human resources, advancement, compliance, and administrative functions. Facilitate process discovery sessions with campus units to document current-state and future-state workflows, process owners, roles, approvals, decision points, handoffs, systems, data inputs and outputs, integrations, controls, pain points, and performance measures. Use standard process-mapping methods and a consistent institutional repository to ensure that documentation is clear, current, reusable, and connected to the enterprise application portfolio. Identify inefficient, duplicative, manual, inconsistent, or system-constrained processes and develop recommendations for simplification, standardization, automation, redesign, or elimination. Ensure ERP and application projects redesign processes where appropriate rather than automatically recreating outdated practices in new technology. Partner with functional leaders to establish accountable process ownership, approve future-state designs, define measurable outcomes, and maintain processes after implementation. Enterprise Systems ( ERP , HCM , and other enterprise applications) Replacement and Transformation Provide IT-side program leadership for the evaluation, selection, planning, implementation, and operational transition of replacement ERP , HCM , and other enterprise applications. Coordinate institutional requirements gathering, fit-gap analysis, business process redesign, solution demonstrations, evaluation criteria, implementation sequencing, integration planning, data readiness, testing, training readiness, cutover, and stabilization activities. Work with IT staff, functional leaders, project managers, vendors, and consultants to establish scope, governance, roles, milestones, dependencies, deliverables, decision processes, and escalation paths. Maintain an integrated view of ERP - and HCM -related risks, issues, decisions, costs, resource needs, competing priorities, and dependencies across functional and technical workstreams. Ensure that ERP and HCM design decisions align with enterprise architecture, data governance, security, accessibility, records management, reporting, integration, and operational support requirements. Plan for the orderly transition from legacy systems, including coexistence, data retention, decommissioning, contract termination, integration replacement, and reduction of dual-system costs and risks. Coordinate the transition from project implementation to sustainable service ownership, support procedures, documentation, monitoring, enhancement governance, and vendor management. Application Integration and Solution Architecture Establish and maintain an inventory of application integrations, interfaces, application programming interfaces (APIs), data exchanges, file transfers, scheduled jobs, middleware components, dependencies, owners, service accounts, and monitoring requirements. Provide governance and design oversight for integrations to improve reliability, security, supportability, documentation, reuse, and alignment with institutional data standards. With the CIO and IT staff, coordinate the design, development, testing, implementation, monitoring, maintenance, and retirement of integrations among enterprise applications. Promote standards-based, maintainable integration approaches and reduce reliance on undocumented, fragile, duplicative, or person-dependent interfaces. Ensure integration requirements address data ownership, system-of-record (Banner) designation, validation, exception handling, reconciliation, logging, access controls, privacy, continuity, and recovery. Coordinate with the CIO and IT staff to resolve cross-system issues and manage architectural dependencies. IT Implementation Plan & Student-Focused Services Support the CIO in translating the institutional IT Implementation Plan into prioritized initiatives, measurable work plans, resource requirements, and sustainable operational practices. Provide leadership for the execution of IT Implementation Plan initiatives assigned to Enterprise Systems and Cybersecurity, coordinating dependencies, risks, budgets, timelines, and cross-functional participation. Ensure enterprise systems, cybersecurity, network, cloud, and infrastructure investments advance institutional priorities and improve the technology experience for students, faculty, and staff. Prioritize reliable and secure student-facing technology services, including wireless connectivity, identity and access services, internet access, classroom and learning-space connectivity, and the infrastructure supporting digital academic and student services. Use service performance data, capacity planning, cybersecurity risk assessments, user feedback, and established lifecycle standards to identify improvement needs and guide technology refresh and modernization decisions. Partner with Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Facilities, and other campus units to anticipate student technology needs and coordinate infrastructure improvements that support learning, engagement, retention, and campus life. Governance, Risk, and Service Management Develop and administer enterprise application governance practices, standards, intake processes, review criteria, decision records, and accountability structures. Partner with the CIO and IT staff to ensure that application decisions address cybersecurity, privacy, regulatory, records, accessibility, continuity, and third-party risk requirements. Establish service ownership, support expectations, escalation procedures, documentation standards, change controls, release coordination, and operational measures for enterprise solutions. Maintain and communicate application risks, mitigation plans, lifecycle concerns, resource constraints, vendor dependencies, and business continuity considerations. Prepare concise executive reports, portfolio dashboards, project status updates, decision briefs, budget requests, risk assessments, and recommendations. Support technology procurement and administrative software acquisition processes by ensuring appropriate technical, security, data, integration, accessibility, support, and lifecycle review. Leadership, Collaboration, and Project Management Build effective working relationships with campus executives, functional leaders, subject-matter experts, IT staff, Procurement, Finance, vendors, and consultants. Develop project work plans, requirements, priorities, schedules, resource plans, risk registers, status reports, and implementation documentation. Communicate complex technical, contractual, operational, and financial issues in clear business terms for the CIO , leadership team, and the Board of Governors. Promote disciplined documentation and knowledge transfer to reduce institutional reliance on individual staff members or vendors. Perform other duties consistent with the responsibilities of the position and the strategic needs of Information Technology Services and Fairmont State University. Perform all other duties as assigned. Employment Conditions Full-time administrative position with responsibilities that may require work outside normal business hours during major implementations, system changes, testing, cutovers, incidents, or other institutional priorities. The position must comply with Fairmont State University policies and applicable requirements governing confidentiality, information security, privacy, records, accessibility, procurement, and acceptable use of technology. Work priorities are established by the CIO and may change according to institutional needs, contractual deadlines, regulatory requirements, and project schedules. Minimum Required Education Bachelor’s degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, Information Systems, or an equivalent combination of education, training, and relevant professional experience. Required Licenses and/or Certifications N/A Minimum Required Qualifications Experience managing enterprise applications, enterprise systems, application portfolios, business systems, or technology programs. Experience in higher education and familiarity with administrative and academic processes across student, finance, human resources, advancement, and institutional reporting functions. Experience with software contracts, renewals, licensing, subscriptions, vendor management, and cost or utilization analysis. Experience gathering requirements and mapping, analyzing, redesigning, or documenting cross-functional business processes. Experience planning, governing, implementing, or supporting application integrations and data exchanges. Ability to lead cross-functional initiatives involving technical teams, business offices, vendors, and consultants. Knowledge of application lifecycle management, solution evaluation, project governance, risk management, testing, implementation, and operational transition. Ability to analyze complex business and technical issues, develop practical recommendations, and manage multiple high-priority initiatives. Strong project management, facilitation, negotiation, documentation, presentation, and written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, maintain confidentiality, and communicate effectively with technical and nontechnical stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in Information Systems, Business Administration, Project Management, Computer Science, or a related field. Experience leading or coordinating an ERP , HCM , enterprise application, digital transformation, or system replacement initiative. Experience with ERP selection, implementation, migration, integration, or replacement in a complex organization. Experience with Software as a Service (SaaS) applications, enterprise architecture, identity and access management, data governance, and systems-of-record governance. Experience developing application inventories, roadmaps, total-cost-of-ownership analyses, renewal calendars, or portfolio dashboards. Experience with business process modeling methods and tools, such as Business Process Model and Notation ( BPMN ), value-stream mapping, swim lane diagrams, or SIPOC . Experience with integration platforms, APIs, middleware, data transformation, batch interfaces, secure file exchange, monitoring, and integration documentation. Experience supporting technology procurement, contract review, information security review, accessibility review, privacy review, records management, and vendor risk assessment. Experience managing organizational change, stakeholder engagement, training readiness, and transition to sustainable operations. Posting Detail Information Requisition Number 2027041SP Number of Vacancies 1 Anticipated Hire Date ASAP Position End Date (if temporary) Job Open Date 11/17/2026 Job Close Date 12/01/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicants Advertising Summary Quicklink for Posting https://www.fairmontstatejobs.com/postings/12159 Job Duties Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter Optional Documents Certification/License Transcript #1 Transcript #2 Transcript #3 Contact Us For questions relating to Faculty/Staff positions, please e-mail the Human Resources Office at HR@fairmontstate.edu or call (304) 367-4111. For questions relating to Student Employment, please e-mail the Student Employment Office at studentjobs@fairmontstate.edu or call (304) 367-4836. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.fairmontstatejobs.com/postings/12198": {
      "title": "Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Systems Coordinator",
      "description": "Fairmont State University Portal | Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Systems Coordinator Skip to Main Content Office of Human Resources 1201 Locust Avenue, Fairmont, West Virginia 26554 Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Systems Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Summary Working Title Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Systems Coordinator Type of Position Staff - Full Time Department Registrar's Office Appointment Length July - June (12 mo) Benefits Eligible? Yes Published Salary Requested Part-Time Staff Hours NA F.T.E. 1.00 Grant Funded Position? No If temporary (or grant funded) position, indicate ending date Description of Job Duties and Responsibilities Fairmont State University is seeking a detail-oriented and collaborative professional to serve as our Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Systems Coordinator. This position plays an important role in supporting the University’s academic operations by coordinating course scheduling processes, maintaining curriculum and academic systems, and ensuring the accuracy and integrity of academic information. Working closely with faculty, academic leadership, and campus partners, the Coordinator helps ensure that academic scheduling and curriculum processes run efficiently and effectively. The ideal candidate is organized, analytical, technologically proficient, and committed to providing excellent service while supporting the University’s mission of student success and academic excellence. At Fairmont State University, we believe our people make the difference. Join a team committed to making Fairmont State A Great Place to Learn, to Work, and to Call Home! Job Duties Include: Academic scheduling: Manage the year-round master course schedule in Banner and Coursedog. Establish and communicate timelines to program directors, chairs, deans, and colleges for administrative review, open scheduling, validation, registration readiness, and scheduling closure. Develop term-roll criteria, validate roll data, and verify appropriate scheduling-report access for academic leaders following each roll. Create, update, and cancel sections and resolve data discrepancies before reporting. Systems administration: Lead functional implementation and ongoing use of assigned Coursedog modules. Maintain approved user access, workflows, and configurations. Coordinate ongoing integration data validation, testing, and troubleshooting with Information Technology and the vendor; and Support reporting and process improvements. Curriculum support: Serve as an ex-officio member of undergraduate and graduate curriculum committees, advising on system implications of proposed changes. Support curriculum submissions and approval workflows, and coordinate implementation of approved changes in Banner, Coursedog, Degree Works, and relevant university webpages with the Registrar, Enrollment Management, and other responsible offices. Catalog maintenance: Share responsibility for reviewing and updating course and program information in Banner and Coursedog, ensuring approved additions, revisions, and deletions are accurate and reflected in the appropriate catalog year and effective term. Course fees: Coordinate with the Bursar, Student Accounts, and Finance to review course fees and implement approved updates in Banner and Coursedog, as applicable. Training and support: Train faculty and staff in scheduling, curriculum submissions, and related system functions. Maintain procedures, user guides, and communications to support consistent practices and timely completion of academic processes. Classroom and event scheduling: Coordinate with Student Affairs and other campus partners to review faculty and staff requests, confirm classroom availability, and maintain reservations and event scheduling records for testing, meetings, conferences, and other approved events. Registrar operations: Process approved major changes for current students, serve as backup to the Transfer Credit Coordinator, and provide shared technical and operational support for the Registrar’s Office and Academic Affairs. Maintain confidentiality and perform related duties as assigned. Minimum Required Education Bachelor’s degree (or equivalent, as determined by HR). Required Licenses and/or Certifications N/A Minimum Required Qualifications Two years of relevant experience; however, any equivalent combination of education and/or experience is acceptable which provides an applicant the listed knowledge, skills, and abilities and the capability to perform the essential functions of the job. Preferred Qualifications Advanced computer skills including database systems Critical thinking skills Ability to work as an integral part of a team to accomplish various projects simultaneously Ability to take initiative and lead Ability to manage competing deadlines, train users, and handle confidential information appropriately Strong organizational, analytical, communication, and collaborative problem-solving skills Flexibility Knowledge of student Information Systems (preferably Banner) Related Higher Education Experience Posting Detail Information Requisition Number 2027043SP Number of Vacancies 1 Anticipated Hire Date ASAP Position End Date (if temporary) Job Open Date 11/20/2026 Job Close Date 12/04/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicants Advertising Summary Quicklink for Posting https://www.fairmontstatejobs.com/postings/12196 Job Duties Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter Optional Documents Certification/License Transcript #1 Transcript #2 Transcript #3 Contact Us For questions relating to Faculty/Staff positions, please e-mail the Human Resources Office at HR@fairmontstate.edu or call (304) 367-4111. For questions relating to Student Employment, please e-mail the Student Employment Office at studentjobs@fairmontstate.edu or call (304) 367-4836. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
